  "id": "kernel-lift",
  "after": "keyTerms",
  "title": "A kernel lifts the data so a straight cut works.",
  "caption": "Crops do well when the monsoon is near normal, and badly in drought or flood. So on the bottom line, no single cut separates good years from poor ones. Add a new feature, (rainfall)², and every point rises onto a U shape. Now one straight SVM line, with the widest gap, separates them; the circled points are the support vectors. Back on the bottom line, that one cut becomes two cuts, at about −18% and +18%. A kernel does this lifting for you, without making the new column by hand.",
  "alt": "Twelve years plotted on an original line of rainfall versus normal: good harvests in the middle and poor harvests at both ends. Above it, each point is lifted to a height of rainfall squared, forming a U shape, and a horizontal SVM line with a shaded widest gap separates good harvests below from poor harvests above; dashed lines show this one line becomes two cuts at −18% and +18% on the original line.",
